  "isLocked": true,
  "description": "Cast designated DataFrame columns to target data types and report converted records and updated schema types.\n\nExplicit dtype casting prevents silent type-coercion bugs and minimizes memory consumption in tabular datasets.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\text{df}[c] = \\text{df}[c].\\operatorname{astype}(\\tau)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Instantiate DataFrame from record dictionaries\n2. Convert designated columns to target dtypes (e.g. float64, int64, str)\n3. Report serialized records and resulting schema dtype mapping\n\n### Returns\nDictionary with 'records' and 'dtypes' mapping.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Change Data Types\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world tabular datasets are frequently messy, containing missing values, duplicates, and unaligned columns. Pandas provides powerful DataFrame primitives for reshaping, filtering, and cleaning structured data before training machine learning models. **Change Data Types** performs structured data manipulation efficiently.\n\nExplicit dtype casting prevents silent type-coercion bugs and minimizes memory consumption in tabular datasets. In this problem, the goal is to: cast designated dataframe columns to target data types and report converted records and updated schema types.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\text{df}[c] = \\text{df}[c].\\operatorname{astype}(\\tau)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Instantiate DataFrame from record dictionaries\n2. **Phase 2**: Convert designated columns to target dtypes (e.g. float64, int64, str)\n3. **Phase 3**: Report serialized records and resulting schema dtype mapping\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Vectorized Array Operations**: Applying vectorized transformations operates on contiguous C memory buffers under the hood, running orders of magnitude faster than iterating with Python loops.\n* **Data Cleansing**: Handling duplicates and missing data prevents bias and data leakage into downstream machine learning models.\n* **Memory Management**: Choosing appropriate column dtypes (e.g. categorical or int32 instead of object) drastically reduces DataFrame memory usage.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: records = [{\"id\": 1, \"val\": \"100\"}, {\"id\": 2, \"val\": \"200\"}], column_types = {\"val\": \"int64\"}\n\nOutput: {\"records\": [{\"id\": 1, \"val\": 100}, {\"id\": 2, \"val\": 200}], \"dtypes\": {\"id\": \"int64\", \"val\": \"int64\"}}\n\nExplanation: Evaluated pandas_change_dtypes under standard test conditions."
  ],
  "requirements": "* Function must be named `pandas_change_dtypes`\n* Return value must match the exact expected structure and types\n* Handle edge cases cleanly and maintain numerical stability\n* Must run efficiently without redundant memory allocations",
  "hints": [
